  "slug": "bifurcation-topic-8fba",
  "summary": "Four new studies tie dendritic, spiking and connectome constraints to network dynamics, showing that matched tuning or outputs can hide distinct underlying regimes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how biophysical and structural constraints at one scale determine dynamics at another. A spatial leaky integrate-and-fire model maps spikes to activity and phase fields, tying turbulence-like dynamics to delayed propagation and dendritic filtering. Deep network surrogates speed a multiscale macaque V1 model about 10^5-fold, enabling stability analysis that shows similar visual tuning can conceal different recovery dynamics. At the single-neuron level, models show dendritic depolarization block supports XOR computation gated by input timing, robust across morphologies. The FlyNet benchmark against CNNs, RAFT and CTRNNs reports efficiency gains on collision detection but adversarial vulnerability; it is a performance comparison rather than a dynamical-regime analysis, so the thread is dominant rather than universal.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
